IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `types`

§ `Chapter 4 -- General exceptions`

-- Penal Code 1871 ss 6, 76, 77, 78, 79, 79A, 80, 81, 82, 83, 84, 85, 86, 87, 88,
-- 89, 90, 91, 92, 93, 94, 95.
-- Every definition of an offence, every penal provision and every illustration is
-- understood subject to these exceptions and to Chapter 4A (s 6).
-- Source: registers/source-bundle/PC1871.txt.

§§ `Section 76 -- Bound or justified by law`

-- Nothing is an offence which is done by a person who is bound by law to do it or
-- justified by law in doing it.
-- Illustration (b): a police officer who apprehends a murderer without warrant is
-- not guilty of wrongful confinement.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 76"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 76 applies` IF
    e's `bound by law to do it or justified by law in doing it`

§§ `Section 77 -- Act of a judge acting judicially`

-- Nothing is an offence which is done by a judge when acting judicially in the
-- exercise of any power which is, or which in good faith he believes to be, given
-- to him by law.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 77"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 77 applies` IF
    e's `is a judge acting judicially in the exercise of a power given or believed in good faith to be given by law`

§§ `Section 78 -- Act done pursuant to the judgment or order of a court`

-- Nothing done in pursuance of, or warranted by, the judgment or order of a court of
-- justice, if done while the judgment or order remains in force, is an offence, even
-- if the court had no jurisdiction to pass it, provided the doer in good faith
-- believes that the court had such jurisdiction.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 78"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 78 applies` IF
        e's `is done in pursuance of or warranted by the judgment or order of a court of justice`
    AND e's `the judgment or order remains in force`
    AND e's `the doer in good faith believes the court had jurisdiction to pass it`

§§ `Section 79 -- Mistake of fact`

-- s 79(1): unless otherwise provided by written law, nothing is an offence which is
-- done by a person who, by reason of a mistake of fact or in ignorance of a fact, in
-- good faith believes himself bound by law to do it or justified by law in doing it.
-- s 79(2) and Explanations 1 and 2: where the alleged mistake may instead negate the
-- fault element, the prosecution must still prove the fault element; the accused
-- need not rely on the defence at all. That is a rule about burden, not a further
-- condition of the exception, so it is not a conjunct here.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 79(1)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 79 applies` IF
        e's `by reason of a mistake of fact or ignorance of a fact believes in good faith he is bound or justified by law`
    AND (NOT e's `another written law otherwise provides`)

§§ `Section 79A -- Mistake of law is not a defence`

-- s 79A(1) is a closure rule, not an exception: a mistake of law or ignorance of the
-- law is never a defence unless another written law so provides. It therefore
-- contributes no limb to `a general exception applies`. s 79A(2) preserves the
-- prosecution's burden on the fault element and is a rule of proof, not of liability.
-- Recorded here so the coverage is visible; nothing is decided.

§§ `Section 80 -- Accident in the doing of a lawful act`

-- s 80(1): nothing is an offence which is done by accident or misfortune in the
-- doing of a lawful act in a lawful manner, by lawful means, and with proper care
-- and caution.
-- Explanation: a lawful act is any act which is not an offence under this Code or
-- any written law and which is not otherwise prohibited by law.
-- s 80(2) is again a rule about the prosecution's burden, not a further condition.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 80(1)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 80 applies` IF
        e's `is done by accident or misfortune`
    AND e's `is the doing of a lawful act in a lawful manner by lawful means`
    AND e's `is done with proper care and caution`

§§ `Section 81 -- Act likely to cause harm but done to prevent other harm`

-- Nothing is an offence merely by reason of its being done with the knowledge that
-- it is likely to cause harm, if it is done in good faith for the purpose of
-- preventing or avoiding other harm to person or property.
-- Explanation: it is a question of fact whether the harm to be prevented was of such
-- a nature and so imminent as to justify or excuse the risk. That question is a
-- conjunct here, because without it the section does not excuse.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 81"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 81 applies` IF
        e's `is done only with knowledge that it is likely to cause harm`
    AND e's `is done in good faith to prevent or avoid other harm to person or property`
    AND e's `the harm to be prevented was of such a nature and so imminent as to justify the risk`

§§ `Section 82 -- Child below 10`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 82"
GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 82 applies` IF
    a's `age in years` LESS THAN 10

§§ `Section 83 -- Child of or above 10 and below 12 without sufficient maturity`

-- Nothing is an offence which is done by a child of or above 10 years and below 12
-- who has not attained sufficient maturity of understanding to judge of the nature
-- and consequence of his conduct on that occasion.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 83"
GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 83 applies` IF
        a's `age in years` AT LEAST 10
    AND a's `age in years` LESS THAN 12
    AND (NOT a's `has sufficient maturity of understanding`)

§§ `Section 84 -- Unsound mind`

-- s 84(1): nothing is an offence which is done by a person who, at the time of doing
-- it, by reason of unsoundness of mind, is (a) incapable of knowing the nature of the
-- act, (b) incapable of knowing that what he is doing is wrong, or (c) completely
-- deprived of any power to control his actions.
-- s 84(2): limb (b) applies only if the person is incapable of knowing that the act
-- is wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons AND wrong as
-- contrary to law. Both are required, so a person who knows the act is contrary to
-- law is outside (b) even if he believes it morally right (the Illustration).

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 84(2)"
GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 84(1)(b) incapacity is made out` IF
        a's `by reason of unsoundness of mind is incapable of knowing the act is wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
    AND a's `by reason of unsoundness of mind is incapable of knowing the act is wrong as contrary to law`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 84(1)"
GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 84 applies` IF
        a's `is of unsound mind at the time of the act`
    AND (   a's `by reason of unsoundness of mind is incapable of knowing the nature of the act`
         OR `the section 84(1)(b) incapacity is made out` a
         OR a's `by reason of unsoundness of mind is completely deprived of any power to control his actions`)

§§ `Section 85 -- Intoxication when a defence`

-- s 85(1): except as provided in s 85 and s 86, intoxication is not a defence.
-- s 85(2): intoxication is a defence if by reason of it the person charged, at the
-- time of the act, (a) did not know what he was doing, or (b) did not know that the
-- act was wrong, AND the state of intoxication was caused without his knowledge or
-- against his will.
-- s 85(2A): limb (b) applies only if he did not know the act was wrong by the
-- ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons AND wrong as contrary to law.
-- s 85(3): intoxication is a defence if by reason of it the person was of unsound
-- mind as determined under s 84. This limb does not carry the "without knowledge or
-- against the will" requirement, which s 85(2) attaches only to its own limbs.
-- s 86(3): "intoxication" includes a state produced by narcotics or drugs.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 85(2A)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 85(2)(b) ignorance is made out` IF
        e's `by reason of intoxication did not know the act was wrong by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
    AND e's `by reason of intoxication did not know the act was wrong as contrary to law`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 85(2)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 85(2) applies` IF
        (   e's `by reason of intoxication did not know what he was doing`
         OR `the section 85(2)(b) ignorance is made out` e)
    AND e's `intoxication caused without the knowledge or against the will of the person charged`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 85"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 85 applies` IF
        `section 85(2) applies` e
    OR  e's `by reason of intoxication was of unsound mind as determined under section 84`

§§ `Section 86 -- Effect of the defence of intoxication when established`

-- s 86(1): where the s 85 defence is established the accused must be acquitted,
-- except that if the person was of unsound mind by reason of intoxication, s 84 of
-- this Code and ss 251 and 252 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 apply -- that is,
-- the disposal is the unsound-mind disposal, not a simple acquittal.
-- s 86(2): intoxication is in any event taken into account in deciding whether the
-- person formed the intention or had the knowledge or belief without which he would
-- not be guilty. That is a rule of proof and adds no limb.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 86(1)"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 84 disposal applies rather than a simple acquittal` IF
        `section 85 applies` e
    AND e's `by reason of intoxication was of unsound mind as determined under section 84`

§§ `Section 90 -- When a consent is not a consent`

-- A consent is not such a consent as is intended by any section of this Code
-- (a) if given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to any person, or under a
-- misconception of fact, and the doer knows or has reason to believe the consent was
-- given in consequence of that fear or misconception; (b) if given by a person who,
-- from unsoundness of mind, mental incapacity, intoxication or the influence of any
-- drug or other substance, is unable to understand its nature and consequence; or
-- (c) unless the contrary appears from the context, if given by a person below 12.
--
-- s 90 governs every consent-based exception, so it is applied inside ss 87 to 89
-- and 92 rather than standing as an exception of its own.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 90"
@export Decide whether a consent is vitiated under Penal Code 1871 section 90
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts` @desc Consent given under fear or misconception known to the doer, or by a person unable to understand its nature and consequence, or by a person below 12 years of age unless the contrary appears from the context.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the consent is not a consent within section 90` IF
        (    (   e's `the consent was given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to any person`
              OR e's `the consent was given under a misconception of fact`)
         AND e's `the doer knows or has reason to believe the consent was given in consequence of that fear or misconception`)
    OR  e's `the consent giver was unable to understand the nature and consequence of it through unsoundness of mind mental incapacity intoxication or drugs`
    OR  (    e's `the consent giver is below 12 years of age`
         AND (NOT e's `a contrary intention as to the age of consent appears from the context`))

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 87, 88, 90"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is an effective consent by the person harmed` IF
        e's `the person harmed has consented expressly or impliedly to suffer that harm or to take the risk of it`
    AND (NOT `the consent is not a consent within section 90` e)

§§ `Section 91 -- Acts which are offences independently of the harm consented to`

-- The exceptions in ss 87, 88 and 89 do not extend to acts which are offences
-- independently of any harm they may cause to the person giving the consent or on
-- whose behalf it is given.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 91"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 91 shuts out the consent exceptions` IF
    e's `the act is an offence independently of any harm to the person consenting`

§§ `Section 87 -- Harm by consent, not intended or known to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt`

-- Nothing which is not intended to cause death or grievous hurt, and which is not
-- known by the doer to be likely to cause death or grievous hurt, is an offence by
-- reason of harm caused or intended to any person above 18 who has consented,
-- expressly or impliedly, to suffer that harm or to take the risk of it.
-- Illustration: two people who agree to fence for amusement consent to any harm
-- caused without foul play.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 87"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 87 applies` IF
        (NOT e's `is intended to cause death`)
    AND (NOT e's `is known by the doer to be likely to cause death`)
    AND (NOT e's `is intended to cause grievous hurt`)
    AND (NOT e's `is known by the doer to be likely to cause grievous hurt`)
    AND e's `the person harmed is above 18 years of age`
    AND `there is an effective consent by the person harmed` e
    AND (NOT `section 91 shuts out the consent exceptions` e)

§§ `Section 88 -- Harm by consent in good faith for the benefit of the person harmed`

-- Nothing which is not intended to cause death is an offence by reason of harm
-- caused, intended or known to be likely, to a person for whose benefit it is done
-- in good faith and who has consented expressly or impliedly to suffer that harm or
-- take the risk of it.
-- Illustration: a surgeon who performs an operation likely to cause death, with the
-- patient's consent and in good faith for the patient's benefit, commits no offence.
-- Explanation to s 92: mere pecuniary benefit is not benefit for ss 88, 89 and 92.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 88"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the act is for the benefit of the person harmed in good faith` IF
        e's `the act is done in good faith for the benefit of the person harmed`
    AND (NOT e's `the benefit relied on is merely pecuniary`)

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 88"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 88 applies` IF
        (NOT e's `is intended to cause death`)
    AND `the act is for the benefit of the person harmed in good faith` e
    AND `there is an effective consent by the person harmed` e
    AND (NOT `section 91 shuts out the consent exceptions` e)

§§ `Section 89 -- Act for the benefit of a child below 12 or a person of unsound mind`

-- Nothing done in good faith for the benefit of a person below 12 years of age, or
-- of unsound mind, by or by the consent (express or implied) of the guardian or
-- other person having lawful charge of that person, is an offence by reason of harm
-- caused, intended or known to be likely to be caused to that person.
-- Proviso (a) to (d): the exception does not extend to the intentional causing of
-- death or an attempt to cause death; to anything known to be likely to cause death
-- for a purpose other than preventing death or grievous hurt or curing a grievous
-- disease or infirmity; to the voluntary causing of grievous hurt or an attempt at
-- it, unless for that same purpose; or to the abetment of an offence to which the
-- exception would not extend.
-- The guardian's consent is itself a consent within s 90, so it is tested there.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 89"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 89 proviso shuts out the exception` IF
        e's `is intended to cause death`
    OR  e's `is an attempt to cause death`
    OR  (    e's `is known by the doer to be likely to cause death`
         AND (NOT e's `the purpose is preventing death or grievous hurt or curing a grievous disease or infirmity`))
    OR  (    e's `is the voluntary causing of grievous hurt or an attempt to cause grievous hurt`
         AND (NOT e's `the purpose is preventing death or grievous hurt or curing a grievous disease or infirmity`))
    OR  e's `is the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 89"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 89 applies` IF
        (   e's `the person harmed is below 12 years of age`
         OR e's `the person harmed is of unsound mind`)
    AND `the act is for the benefit of the person harmed in good faith` e
    AND (   e's `the guardian or person having lawful charge has consented expressly or impliedly`
         OR e's `bound by law to do it or justified by law in doing it`)
    AND (NOT `a section 89 proviso shuts out the exception` e)
    AND (NOT `section 91 shuts out the consent exceptions` e)

§§ `Section 92 -- Act for a person benefit without consent`

-- Nothing is an offence by reason of harm caused to a person for whose benefit it is
-- done in good faith, even without that person's consent, if it is impossible for
-- that person to signify consent, or he is incapable of giving it and has no
-- guardian or other person in lawful charge from whom consent can be obtained in
-- time for the thing to be done with benefit.
-- Proviso (a) to (d): does not extend to the intentional causing of death or an
-- attempt at it; to anything known to be likely to cause death for a purpose other
-- than preventing death or grievous hurt or curing a grievous disease or infirmity;
-- to the voluntary causing of hurt or an attempt at it for a purpose other than
-- preventing death or hurt; or to the abetment of an offence to which it would not
-- extend.
-- Note the proviso differs from s 89: here limb (c) is hurt, not grievous hurt, and
-- its saving purpose is "preventing death or hurt".

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 92"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 92 proviso shuts out the exception` IF
        e's `is intended to cause death`
    OR  e's `is an attempt to cause death`
    OR  (    e's `is known by the doer to be likely to cause death`
         AND (NOT e's `the purpose is preventing death or grievous hurt or curing a grievous disease or infirmity`))
    OR  (    e's `is the voluntary causing of hurt or an attempt to cause hurt`
         AND (NOT e's `the purpose is preventing death or hurt`))
    OR  e's `is the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 92"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 92 applies` IF
        `the act is for the benefit of the person harmed in good faith` e
    AND e's `it is impossible for the person harmed to signify consent or he is incapable of giving it`
    AND e's `there is no guardian or person in lawful charge from whom consent can be obtained in time`
    AND (NOT `a section 92 proviso shuts out the exception` e)

§§ `Section 93 -- Communication made in good faith`

-- No communication made in good faith is an offence by reason of any harm to the
-- person to whom it is made, if it is made for the benefit of that person.
-- Illustration: a surgeon who in good faith tells a patient he cannot live commits
-- no offence though the patient dies of the shock.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 93"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 93 applies` IF
    e's `is a communication made in good faith for the benefit of the person to whom it is made`

§§ `Section 94 -- Act compelled by threats`

-- Except murder and offences against the State punishable with death, nothing is an
-- offence which is done by a person compelled to do it by threats which, at the time
-- of doing it, reasonably cause the apprehension that instant death to that person or
-- any other person will otherwise be the consequence.
-- Proviso: not available where the doer placed himself in that situation of his own
-- accord, or from a reasonable apprehension of harm to himself short of instant
-- death (Explanation 1: joining gang-robbers knowing their character).

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 94"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 94 applies` IF
        e's `is compelled by threats reasonably causing apprehension of instant death to that person or another`
    AND (NOT e's `the doer placed himself in that situation of his own accord or from apprehension of harm short of instant death`)
    AND (NOT e's `the offence is murder or an offence against the State punishable with death`)

§§ `Section 95 -- Slight harm`

-- Nothing is an offence by reason that it causes, is intended to cause, or is known
-- to be likely to cause any harm, if that harm is so slight that no person of
-- ordinary sense and temper would complain of it.

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 95"
GIVEN e IS AN `Exception Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 95 applies` IF
    e's `harm is so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain`

§§ `A general exception applies`

@ref "Penal Code 1871, ss 6 and 76 to 95"
@export Decide whether a Chapter 4 general exception of the Penal Code 1871 means the act is not an offence
GIVEN a IS AN Actor @desc The actor. Age in years and sufficient maturity carry ss 82 and 83; the unsoundness-of-mind facts carry s 84.
      e IS AN `Exception Facts` @desc The exception facts. Set only the flags true of the act; the rest stay false. Consent-based exceptions are already filtered through s 90 and s 91.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a general exception applies to` a e IF
        `section 76 applies` e
    OR  `section 77 applies` e
    OR  `section 78 applies` e
    OR  `section 79 applies` e
    OR  `section 80 applies` e
    OR  `section 81 applies` e
    OR  `section 82 applies` a
    OR  `section 83 applies` a
    OR  `section 84 applies` a
    OR  `section 85 applies` e
    OR  `section 87 applies` e
    OR  `section 88 applies` e
    OR  `section 89 applies` e
    OR  `section 92 applies` e
    OR  `section 93 applies` e
    OR  `section 94 applies` e
    OR  `section 95 applies` e

@ref "Penal Code 1871, s 6"
@export Decide whether a Chapter 4 general exception of the Penal Code 1871 means the proposed act is not an offence
GIVEN p IS A `Proposed Act` @desc The proposed act. Its actor and exception facts carry the whole of Chapter 4.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a general exception applies` IF
    `a general exception applies to` (p's `actor`) (p's `exception facts`)
